<BaseLayout title="Engine Selection" description="Choosing the right LS engine for a Hyundai Genesis Coupe swap — cost, weight, and community experience.">
  <h1>Engine Selection</h1>
  <p>
    Almost any Gen III/IV LS will physically fit. The real decisions are budget, weight, power goals, and what transmission you pair it with.
  </p>

  <h2>Common Choices</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>5.3 / LQ4 / LQ9 (iron):</strong> Cheap, tough, easy to find. Heavier than aluminum. Popular for budget and boosted builds.</li>
    <li><strong>LS1 / LS6:</strong> Aluminum, proven, strong aftermarket. Good all-around street choice.</li>
    <li><strong>LS2 / L92 / LS3-style:</strong> More displacement and power potential; L92 is often described as an "LS3 in truck form" and a strong starting point.</li>
    <li><strong>LS7:</strong> High power out of the box but more expensive and has known valvetrain considerations — research thoroughly.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Cost Reality (Community Reports)</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>True bare-bones DIY with a lucky junkyard deal has been reported under $2k–$3k for engine + trans + basic mounts — rare and usually incomplete vs a "finished daily."</li>
    <li>More typical DIY complete swaps land roughly <strong>$8k–$15k+</strong> depending on transmission, harness/ECU, driveshaft, cooling, and whether labor is DIY.</li>
    <li>Shop-built swaps are higher still. Reddit consensus: treat $10k as a realistic planning floor for a solid street car, not a fantasy minimum.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Weight &amp; Balance</h2>
  <p>
    Iron blocks add nose weight versus the original 2.0T or 3.8. Aluminum LS engines help, but you are still adding two cylinders and related mass.
    Reinforce or upgrade the rear subframe / bushings if you plan hard launches; sedan axles are a common next step (see Transmission).
  </p>

  <h2>What to Buy With the Engine</h2>
  <p>
    Prefer packages that include harness, PCM (even if you later go Holley), accessories, and ideally the pedal if the donor was DBW.
    Video of the engine running and an oil inspection for metal are worth the time before money changes hands.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>
